From: the SK-D840N 7.3 line
Subject: [PATCH] net: zx279133: quarantine a TX ring whose completion counter
 cannot be trusted

The 7.3 tree's driver took a TX completion counter at its word.  When the
counter reported more completions than the ring had frames in it, the
driver clamped the delta to the number of pending frames and carried on:
the slots were released, the DMA mappings were unmapped, and the ring's
done counter was set to the hardware's own value.

An untrusted counter cannot prove that the DMA has given the buffers back.
Releasing a buffer the DMA may still own is a use-after-free that no
counter can rule out, and setting done to the hardware's value throws away
the only baseline that says what was outstanding.

The 6.18 generation of this driver does not believe the counter either.  It
quarantines the ring, stops both logical TX devices, and hands the ring to
a recovery worker that flushes it, reclaims it, and - once - restarts the
shared datapath.  That is what this patch moves to the 7.3 tree.

The model is unchanged in shape and the tree's own datapath skeleton
already has the pieces it needs: zx279133_datapath_suspend() and
zx279133_datapath_resume() exist, and the LAN service already has
zx279133_lan_datapath_get() and zx279133_lan_datapath_put().  What the
tree had was the datapath, not the quarantine.

Four things change:

  * a ring whose counter cannot be trusted is not reclaimed at all, and it
    is not reclaimed again until the datapath has been restarted;
  * the fault stops both logical TX devices, because they share the ring;
  * the recovery worker runs outside RTNL and restarts the datapath at
    most once per real completion - a watchdog that keeps firing must not
    turn into a reset loop;
  * a datapath that could not be brought back up is marked failed, and the
    next user that opens an interface restores it before using it.

The counters are new too: requests, restarts, failures and the invalid
completions themselves.

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-idm.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-idm.c
index e1eb0ca..2b33e0f 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-idm.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-idm.c
@@ -1281,20 +1281,38 @@ unsigned int zx279133_idm_tx_reclaim_queue_locked(struct zx279133_eth *eth,
 {
 	struct zx279133_tx_ring *tx = &eth->tx[queue];
 	u32 done_reg = zx279133_idm_tx_done_reg(ZX279133_IDM_CPU_TX_FIRST + queue);
-	u16 done = readl(eth->base + ZX279133_IDM_BASE + done_reg) & 0xffff;
-	u16 completed = done - tx->done;
+	u16 done;
+	u16 completed;
 	u16 reclaimed;
 	u32 bytes[2] = {};
 	u16 packets[2] = {};
 	u16 xsk_completed = 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * A queue whose completion counter cannot be trusted is not
+	 * reclaimed at all.  Its mappings stay where they are until the
+	 * hardware is stopped: freeing a buffer that the DMA may still own
+	 * is a use-after-free that no counter can rule out.
+	 */
+	if (!READ_ONCE(eth->tx_prepared) ||
+	    !READ_ONCE(eth->hardware_prepared) ||
+	    test_bit(queue, &eth->tx_fault_queues))
+		return 0;
+
+	done = readl(eth->base + ZX279133_IDM_BASE + done_reg) & 0xffff;
+	completed = done - tx->done;
 	eth->tx_reclaim_polls++;
 	if (completed > tx->pending) {
 		if (net_ratelimit())
 			netdev_warn(eth->ndev,
 				    "TX queue %u: invalid completion delta %u (pending %u)\n",
 				    queue, completed, tx->pending);
-		completed = tx->pending;
+		/* An untrusted counter cannot prove DMA ownership has returned.
+		 * Keep the mappings and the old baseline until the hardware is
+		 * stopped, and quarantine the queue.
+		 */
+		zx279133_tx_recovery_request_locked(eth, queue, true);
+		return 0;
 	}
 	tx->done = done;
 	reclaimed = completed;
